  It disappears in: b, BLAS on 1 thread, FAISS on all (+4.4 ms at 100,000 and +5.4 ms at 200,000 and +4.5 ms at 500,000 and +4.7 ms at 1,000,000); f, as a, OpenBLAS's waiting threads passive (+2.6 ms at 100,000 and +4.7 ms at 200,000 and +4.3 ms at 500,000 and +7.8 ms at 1,000,000); h, as a, trying fix (i): the query rotated without BLAS (+4.3 ms at 100,000 and +4.0 ms at 200,000 and +3.5 ms at 500,000 and +4.5 ms at 1,000,000).
  It shrinks to between a quarter and three quarters in: none.
  It stays in: c, FAISS on 1 thread, BLAS on all (+13.0 ms at 100,000 and +2.2 ms at 200,000 and +28.7 ms at 500,000 and +27.1 ms at 1,000,000); e, as a, OpenMP's waiting threads passive (+1.7 ms at 100,000 and +2.4 ms at 200,000 and +23.9 ms at 500,000 and +26.6 ms at 1,000,000); g, as a, trying fix (ii): FAISS calls on 1 thread (+1.6 ms at 100,000 and +11.6 ms at 200,000 and +27.2 ms at 500,000 and +26.9 ms at 1,000,000).
  So it comes from numpy's BLAS threads (the rotation's product). OpenBLAS's waiting threads passive removes it as well: OpenBLAS's threads spin and hold cores.
  Measured here: fix (ii) the compact search's FAISS calls on one OpenMP thread leaves +1.6 ms at 100,000 and +11.6 ms at 200,000 and +27.2 ms at 500,000 and +26.9 ms at 1,000,000 (g: stays); fix (i) the query row rotated without the BLAS leaves +4.3 ms at 100,000 and +4.0 ms at 200,000 and +3.5 ms at 500,000 and +4.5 ms at 1,000,000 (h: gone).
  The fix that applies: (i) the query row rotated without the BLAS. It must also leave the hits the same and the build time as it was (both above).
DISK: yes. Queries asked for the first time take, on all cores (i against a), +14.5 ms at 100,000 and +18.3 ms at 200,000 and +9.5 ms at 500,000 and +3.9 ms at 1,000,000, and on one thread (j against d) -0.0 ms at 100,000 and +0.3 ms at 200,000 and +0.4 ms at 500,000 and -1.0 ms at 1,000,000; in reading the rows +0.2 ms and +0.0 ms and +0.4 ms and +0.2 ms (i). The index file is not all in memory here, so a first search of a query reads rows from the disk, whatever the thread count. bench_search_scale.py times each query the first time it is asked, so its "search ms" includes these reads on such a machine: read its numbers (last night's too) against this line.
=> On this machine both: threads: numpy's BLAS threads, in the fine ranking (+25.7 ms), the coarse scan (+11.2 ms); fix (i) the query row rotated without the BLAS applies; and reads from the disk: a query's first search reads its rows from the disk, whatever the threads.
